<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Keta is a coastal town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a> Region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, on the sandbar between the Atlantic and the Keta lagoon. A trading centre of the Anlo Ewe, it was the site of the Danish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-prinzenstein">Fort Prinzenstein</a> from 1784 and later a busy port under British rule. Much of the town has been lost to sea erosion since the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/keta.jpg" alt="Keta" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Beth Knittle · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Keta_beach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Keta was an important trading post from the 14th century to the late 20th. The Somey Ewe were the first people of the area. The Anlo Ewe, who in the 17th century migrated from Ketu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> by way of Adja Tado and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notsie">Notsie</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>, settled it: according to tradition Awanyedor and Akaga, sons of Torgbui Wenya, the founder of the Anlo State, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anloga">Anloga</a> to found Keta as a fishing village. The town was also known as Quittah or Agudzeawo, meaning Easterners in old Ewe.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, who hoped to trade there without interference from rival European nations, first set up a factory to sell alcohol and, after the Anlo-Danish War, the Sagbadre War of 1784, built a fort whose trade made Keta flourish, though their power reached no further than its guns. In June 1790 a Danish official who had killed the chief of Keta years before was killed near Denu. The Danes hired the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anloga">Anloga</a> to punish Keta; a staged fight became a real one, and a war between Keta and Anloga in 1794 ended in Keta's defeat and weakened the Anlo.</p><p>Missionaries of the North German Missionary Society landed at nearby Dzelukofe on 2 September 1853. From 1874 the Hausa Constabulary was based at Keta, and a community of Hausa traders grew up. In 1887, when the writer R. Austin Freeman was a medical officer there, an epidemic of black water fever killed forty per cent of the European population. The first Orange Lodge in Ghana, Pride of Keta, was founded in 1917 by R. E. Sharlley, a postal worker.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Keta, a town of 23,207 people, is the capital of the Keta Municipal District. Since the early 20th century it has suffered tidal waves, flooding and erosion: the Bremen Factory and a coconut plantation near the high-water mark in 1907 had been swept away by 1924, and the sea has reached Queen Street and the fort. The Keta Lagoon, the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> with 300 km2 of water, lies in a protected wetland and is a stopping point for migratory birds and a breeding ground for sea turtles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Keta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under their leader Sri I the Anlo settled the sandbar between the sea and the <b>Keta</b> lagoon and made Anloga their capital, gradually becoming the most important of the coastal Ewe states.</blockquote><a href="/state/anlo">Anlo</a></li></ul></section>
